## Where it goes

Not on the theme. `$theme->apiAction()` is retired — `NTDST_Theme::__call()` throws `BadMethodCallException` for it. The one path is `ntdst_actions()->register()`, called from the `init()` of the service that owns the artwork model.

Don't make this its own service. A single AJAX action is a sub-component of the feature that already registers the `artwork` model.

## Registration

```php
declare(strict_types=1);

// Inside ArtworkService::init()
ntdst_actions()->register('delete_artwork', function ($data, $params) {
    $id = absint($params['id'] ?? 0);

    if ($id === 0) {
        return new WP_Error('missing_id', 'Artwork id required', ['status' => 400]);
    }

    // The floor was derived from the 'artwork' type, but the id is caller-supplied.
    // Without this, an artwork-gated caller can delete a post of another type.
    if (get_post_type($id) !== 'artwork') {
        return new WP_Error('not_found', 'No such artwork', ['status' => 404]);
    }

    // Per-row meta capability — the floor is alongside this check, never instead of it.
    if (! current_user_can('delete_post', $id)) {
        return new WP_Error('forbidden', 'Not allowed', ['status' => 403]);
    }

    $result = ntdst_data()->get('artwork')->delete($id);

    if (is_wp_error($result)) {
        return $result;
    }

    return ['deleted' => $id];
}, ['cap_type' => 'artwork']);
```

**`cap_type`, not `capability`.** `ntdst_api_floor_cap()` derives the floor from the post-type object, so a per-type capability map narrows the gate with you. A literal `'delete_others_posts'` is only correct while `artwork`'s `capability_type` is still `'post'`. Never use the `delete_posts` / `edit_posts` family as the gate — Contributors and Authors hold those, and they mean "my own posts". That is exactly the Author case you want to block.

No `'public' => true`. The action stays off `ntdst/api/public_actions`, so the router refuses anonymous callers before dispatch. The floor bites at dispatch, ahead of your handler, and fails closed — an unresolvable capability denies everyone, administrators included.

## Rate limit it

The default is 30/60s. A destructive action deserves less:

```php
add_filter('ntdst/api/rate_limit/delete_artwork', fn() => 10);
add_filter('ntdst/api/rate_window/delete_artwork', fn() => 60);
```

Budget arithmetic: `get_nonce` charges the **target** action's bucket. A cold call costs 2 units, later calls with a cached nonce cost 1.

## Admin JS

There is no admin variant of `$theme->script()`. Use `on()`:

```php
$theme->on('admin_enqueue_scripts', function () {
    ntdst_enqueue_api_client(); // localizes window.ntdstAPIConfig.restNonce
    wp_enqueue_script('artwork-admin', $url, ['ntdst-api'], '1.0', true);
});
```

```javascript
ntdstAPI.call('delete_artwork', { id: 123 })
    .then(data => console.log('deleted', data.deleted))
    .catch(err => console.error(err.message));
```

The client exposes only `call`, `upload`, `download`. It self-skips when `window.ntdstAPI` already exists, so loading it beside a theme bundle is harmless.

## Three traps

- **Return `WP_Error`, never an error array.** The router turns `WP_Error` into `{success:false}` with the real 4xx. A returned array rides out as HTTP 200 `success:true`.
- **`verifyOrigin()` fails open** with no `Origin`, no `Referer` and no auth cookie. It defends a logged-in browser against CSRF; it authenticates nothing. Your capability checks are the real gate.
- **Don't call `ntdst_actions()->clear_post_cache()`.** It is deleted, along with the whole query-cache surface. Core invalidates on save and delete.
